data:
The provided image is not an EEG spectrogram but rather an EEG raw signal montage, showing time-domain waveforms across multiple scalp electrodes over a 300-second period. In this display, all channels exhibit synchronized, rhythmic oscillations with consistent amplitude and frequency — characteristic of normal brain activity during wakefulness or light sleep (e.g., alpha or theta rhythms). There are no signs of epileptic discharges such as: 

- Sudden, high-amplitude spikes or sharp waves; 
- Polyspikes or spike-and-wave complexes; 
- Paroxysmal bursts with abrupt onset and termination; 
- Asymmetrical patterns that would suggest focal seizure activity.

The waveforms are smooth and regular across all channels, indicating a lack of pathological electrical discharges. Therefore, this EEG recording is classified as non-epileptic.
